 different states :
	 	    
			enum cpdma_chan_state {
				CPDMA_STATE_IDLE,
				CPDMA_STATE_ACTIVE,
				CPDMA_STATE_STOPPING,
				CPDMA_STATE_ERROR
			};
			
| State      | Meaning                                                              |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `IDLE`     | Channel exists but not started; cannot submit descriptors            |
| `ACTIVE`   | Channel is initialized and ready; DMA engine can process descriptors |
| `STOPPING` | Channel is being torn down; submitting packets is unsafe             |
| `ERROR`    | Hardware or software error occurred; channel unusable                |






5️⃣ Lifecycle of a DMA channel

	Allocation / init (cpdma_chan_alloc()):

	Allocate struct cpdma_chan

	Allocate descriptor rings

	Set state = IDLE
	

Activation (cpdma_chan_start()):

	Enable hardware DMA engine

	Set state = ACTIVE
	
	

Submit packets (cpdma_chan_submit()):

	Only allowed when state = ACTIVE
	
	

Teardown / stop (cpdma_chan_stop()):

	Set state = STOPPING

	Drain in-flight descriptors

	Free descriptors

	Set state = IDLE

	Error / cleanup:

	Set state = ERROR

	Prevent future submission

What is "teardown of a channel"?

Teardown is the process of cleanly stopping a DMA channel.

It usually involves:

Stop hardware DMA engine:

Prevent more descriptors from being fetched

Drain pending descriptors:

Complete or cancel descriptors in-flight

Free descriptor ring memory:

Avoid leaks

Unmap any DMA buffers:

If not zero-copy

Mark channel state as stopped/idle:

So driver knows no more submissions are allowed

Visual analogy:
DMA channel (ACTIVE) ---> Packets moving ---->
          |
          | Teardown
          V
DMA channel STOPPING ---> Flush queue ---> Release resources ---> IDLE

4️⃣ Why check chan->state != ACTIVE before submitting?
spin_lock_irqsave(&chan->lock, flags);
if (chan->state != CPDMA_STATE_ACTIVE) {
    spin_unlock_irqrestore(&chan->lock, flags);
    return -EINVAL;
}


Safety: Don’t allow submission if channel is stopping or idle

Consistency: Hardware should not fetch a descriptor from an invalid channel

Error handling: Returning -EINVAL tells upper layers “channel not usable”

